(25-6 segments without reactive gliosis (27), loss and 
degeneration of nerve cells with marked gliosis in 
the medial motor nuclei of the anterior horn with 
symmetric degeneration in the anterior columns bi- 
laterally (28), and perivascular gliosis (15). The fifth 
patient had a lymphocytic perivascular infiltrate 
predominantly in the spinal cord, brainstem, and 
basal ganglia (15); however, this patient had ex- 
trapyramidal and brainstem ocular signs in the last 2 
years of her life (15), which suggested an additional 
diagnosis of encephalomyelitis. None of these pa- 
tients had an associated hyperkinetic paroxysmal 
movement disorder, and no patient with uncompli- 
cated SPS had perivascular infiltrates, which are 
the hallmarks of encephalomyelitis. 
The syndrome of subacute encephalomyelitis 
with rigidity (16,18,22,23) should be distinguished 
from SPS because of the rapidity of its course. It is 
more similar to the subacute myoclonic spinal neu- 
ronitis described in three patients in 1956 (29) that 
consisted of stimulus-precipitated involuntary pain- 
ful contraction of lower limb, truncal, and, in two of 
the three cases, respiratory muscles. Lymphocytic 
cuffing of intramedullary blood vessels and micro- 
glial proliferation surrounding degenerating neurons 
in the gray matter of the thoracic and lumbar spinal 
cord were seen. 
Two of five patients with SPS reported recently 
had prominent reflex muscle jerking (17); of these, 
one had additional findings, including a supranu- 
clear gaze palsy, suggesting a diagnosis of progres- 
sive encephalomyelitis with rigidity. Tests for anti- 
glutamic acid decarboxylase antibodies were posi- 
tive in this patient, suggesting a possible overlap 
between the two conditions. There have been pa- 
tients with encephalomyelitis who have met the cri- 
teria for SPS but differed in the presence of Babin- 
ski signs. Some of these patients have had antibod- 
ies to glutamic acid decarboxylase (30). 
We considered the hypothesis that our patient ex- 
emplified the development of encephalomyelitis in a 
patient with SPS because of the intermediate dura- 
tion of her illness, between that of SPS alone and 
that of encephalomyelitis. If we were to assume she 
had encephalomyelitis with rigidity from the onset, 
the time course would be the longest ever described 
for that condition. At presentation, she fulfilled 
clinical criteria for SPS (1,2). She did not have Bab- 
inski signs. Supportive evidence for this diagnosis 
was the absence of lumbar lordosis in plain radio- 
graphs of the spine early in the course, the collapse 
of L4 and L2 vertebrae (thought to have been the 
result of lumbar paraspinal muscle spasms), and the 
presence of chronic inflammatory cells in the pan- 
creas (some associated with islets) consistent with a 
reaction to anti-islet cell antibodies. Sudden death, 
as experienced by our patient, has been reported in 
patients with SPS and autonomic instability (15). 
Unusual autonomic responses in our patient in- 
cluded elevation of heart rate and respiratory rate 
when standing compared to supine with no addi- 
tional increase in heart rate during the stressful par- 
oxysmal spell of leg jerking and apnea. The lack of 
testing for anti-glutamic acid decarboxylase anti- 
bodies in this patient is a recognized limitation; 
however, not all patients with SPS have anti- 
glutamic acid decarboxylase antibodies. 
However, one of the patient’s clinical features at 
presentation was atypical for SPS (21), although not 
inconsistent with this diagnosis (1,2): Onset was in 
the distal lower extremity muscles (foot pain, curl- 
ing up of toes) rather than in axial muscles. Proxi- 
mal lower extremity muscle involvement occurred 
gradually, and truncal involvement was late. More- 
over, the neuropathologic findings were those of 
encephalomyelitis, characterized by perivascular 
infiltrates at multiple levels of the neuraxis. The 
focal nature of the abnormalities makes it unlikely 
that these were nonspecific changes due to the 8 
days on a respirator. Although we considered the 
presence of paraneoplastic SPS (3 1) or encephalo- 
myelitis (24,25), no cancer was found at autopsy in 
our patient; in contrast, it was overt in patients de- 
scribed previously with paraneoplastic encephalo- 
myelitis. Moreover, pathologic involvement of the 
anterior horns by paraneoplastic encephalomyelitis 
has been invariably associated with lower motor 
neuron loss and with clinical evidence of lower mo- 
tor neuron disease (24,25), whereas our patient had 
lymphocytic infiltrates in the anterior horns with 
relative preservation of the large motor neurons. 
The CSF results do not help in classifying our pa- 
tient’s condition, either at initial presentation or af- 
ter the development of the jerking spells. A normal 
CSF is the rule in SPS, as was the case when our 
patient first presented. The second CSF examina- 
tion did not show any changes specific for enceph- 
alomyelitis; however, the CSF may be normal in 
encephalomyelitis. It is tantalizing to speculate that 
the increase in serum and CSF oligoclonal bands 
(from one to four) reflects the appearance of spe- 
cific antibodies responsible for the patient’s disease 
progression. However, because samples were not 
saved, this hypothesis cannot be tested. 
